sections.text:
Powers of Chief Registrar

17The Chief Registrar of Deeds or other person designated by Service New Brunswick may:

(a)
make a personal inspection of the building in which any registry office is kept, and of the books, deeds, titles, and instruments in any registry office;

(b)
examine and see that the proper books are provided; that they are in good order and condition; that the proper entries and registration are made therein in a proper manner, and in a due and proper form and order; that the indexes are properly kept; that all instruments are duly endorsed, certified and preserved;

(c)
ascertain if the office is kept duly open at and for the proper times, and that it is at all times duly attended by the registrar or his deputy;

(d)
inform the registrar how and in what manner he shall do any particular act, or amend or correct whatever he may find amiss; and in case he finds the work improperly done by a registrar he has power to order a new book or books to be prepared and completed by the registrar at the latter’s own expense; but the registrar is not bound to carry out instructions in reference to a matter involving a question of legal liability of the registrar to the public or to individuals for the non-performance, improper performance, omission or neglect of any duty or obligation;

(e)
report upon all vacancies by death or otherwise in the office of registrar or deputy registrar;

(f)
inspect all abstract and alphabetical indexes, and settle and certify the sums, if any chargeable therefor;

(g)
fix or settle upon a uniform device for the official seals of the registrars, and see that the registrars are supplied therewith.

